\title{The Computational Complexity of Recipes:\\ A Query-Theoretic Model of Cooking and Tasting}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{24 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
The slogan ``a recipe is an algorithm, so the cost of cooking versus the cost of tasting is
$\Pclass$ versus $\NP$'' is usually left as a metaphor. We make it a theorem. We fix a computational
model --- the deterministic adaptive decision-tree (query) model on Boolean pantries --- in which
cooking means handling all $n$ ingredients and tasting means adaptively probing individual
ingredients of the finished dish. Within this model we prove: tasting is never more expensive than
cooking, $V(R)\le C(R)$; a sensitivity lower bound on tasting time; an \emph{unconditional}
separation of deterministic from nondeterministic verification, realised by the ``some ingredient is
spoiled'' dish, which admits one-probe badness certificates yet requires $n$ probes
deterministically; a two-sided certificate hardness theorem for the parity (``soufflé'') dish, whose
every certificate at every pantry is the entire pantry; full realisation of the verification
spectrum, so that every ratio $C/V=n/k$ occurs; an \emph{inversion} of the naive conjecture ---
break-even recipes $C=V$ are exactly the evasive, maximally hard dishes, while the easiest dishes
attain the extreme ratio $C/V=n$; a counting theorem showing that at most $(6n)^{2^d}$ of the
$2^{2^n}$ dishes are tastable in $d$ probes, so that almost every recipe is a soufflé; an
unconditional adaptivity gap; and a product bound $V(f)\le C_0(f)\,C_1(f)$ --- in the kitchen,
$\NP\cap\coNP$ collapses to $\Pclass$ at the cost of squaring. We also carry out, exactly, a census
of one hundred recipes whose aggregate cook/taste ratio is $200/101$. We explicitly do \emph{not}
claim any Navier--Stokes or $\mathrm{PSPACE}$ content for the soufflé: the honest combinatorial
shadow of that claim is the two-sided certificate theorem.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{query complexity, decision trees, sensitivity, certificate complexity, evasiveness,
adaptivity gap, P vs NP}

